# Telegram Media Mirror & Backup Bot

A private Telegram control bot that backs up **media and files only** from one permitted group/channel to another. It downloads source media to controlled temporary storage and uploads a new destination message. It does **not** call Telegram message forwarding APIs.

## Included baseline

- Python, Telethon, aiogram, SQLite WAL mode
- One encrypted `StringSession` worker-account profile per authorised operator
- `/start`, `/connect`, `/new`, `/projects`, `/help`
- Setup wizard: source, destination, project name, full/from-message/date-range/new-files scan mode
- Source/history and destination-media-permission preflight
- Media-type controls, captions off by default, album option, filename retention
- Ordered oldest-to-newest scanning
- Download → re-upload as new message; no source sender/reply/forward metadata
- Transactional per-message transfer ledger and duplicate protection
- Pause, stop, resume-after-restart, retryable transfer recovery
- FloodWait compliance, bounded transient retries, periodic status updates
- Markdown full report and failed-files view
- Temporary-media cleanup after each upload and startup recovery cleanup
- Docker, Docker Compose, and Render Background Worker configuration

## Runtime model

```text
Owner Telegram account
    │ commands / menus
    ▼
Control bot (BotFather token)
    │
    ▼
SQLite project & transfer ledger ───── encrypted StringSession
    │                                          │
    └──────────── Backup worker ──────────────┘
               │
Source media → temporary disk → fresh upload → destination
```

## Setup

### 1. Prepare configuration

```bash
cd telegram-media-mirror-bot
cp .env.example .env
python -c "from cryptography.fernet import Fernet; print(Fernet.generate_key().decode())"
```

Fill every `put_...` value in `.env`. `OWNER_IDS` is a comma-separated list of numeric Telegram user IDs allowed to use the control bot.

### 2. Local Python setup

```bash
python -m venv .venv
source .venv/bin/activate  # Windows: .venv\Scripts\activate
pip install -e '.[dev]'
pytest -q
python -m app.main
```

Open the bot’s private chat and use `/start`, then **Connect Worker Account**. The initial connection creates an encrypted Telethon StringSession in SQLite. Next use **New Backup Project**.

### 3. Docker Compose

```bash
cp .env.example .env
# edit .env
mkdir -p data/tmp data/reports data/sessions
chmod 700 data data/tmp data/reports data/sessions
docker compose up -d --build
docker compose logs -f
```

The durable `./data` volume contains SQLite metadata/reports. Temporary media lives under `data/tmp` and is removed after a successful transfer.

## Operational behavior

### No forwarding

The worker's transfer route is:

```text
source message → media filter → duplicate ledger check → temporary download
→ client.send_file(destination, ...) → transfer record → temporary-file delete
```

It never invokes `forward_messages`. Caption copy is a project toggle; text-only messages are skipped.

### Ordering and albums

A project uses one ordered worker. It scans `iter_messages(..., reverse=True)` and processes oldest to newest. When album preservation is enabled, contiguous Telegram media groups are uploaded in batches of up to 10 items. If an album operation cannot be completed, each source item is recorded as failed/retryable rather than silently lost.

### Resume and duplicates

`transfer_items` has a unique key `(project_id, source_chat_id, source_message_id)`. Completed entries are not uploaded again when duplicate skipping is enabled. Incomplete items are converted to `RETRY_WAIT` at startup and retried before new messages after the saved checkpoint. Pause/stop take effect at a safe message/album boundary.

### Continuous sync

Set **Continuous sync** in a project’s Settings. After each complete history pass it waits for `SYNC_POLL_SECONDS` (300 by default), then scans messages after its saved checkpoint.

## Commands

| Command | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `/start` | Main menu |
| `/connect` | Connect/reconnect the Telegram worker account |
| `/new` | Start project wizard |
| `/projects` | List projects |
| `/help` | Command summary |

Project cards include **Start/Resume**, **Pause**, **Stop**, **Status**, **Settings**, **Full Report**, **Failed Files**, and **Delete**.

## Testing

```bash
pytest -q
ruff check app tests
python -m compileall -q app
```

The included tests cover SQLite transfer ledger persistence/recovery, settings serialization, safe filenames, and report creation. A real Telegram integration test needs valid app/session credentials and two private test chats. Test these cases before a production archive:

1. document, archive, photo, video, audio, voice, video note, captioned file, and album;
2. text-only/reply/service messages (all excluded);
3. an interrupted run and restart;
4. duplicate/re-run behavior;
5. a destination with revoked media posting permission;
6. new item detection in continuous sync;
7. expected maximum file size.

## Render deployment

This project ships with `render.yaml` configured as a **Background Worker** (not a web service):

1. Create a new Render Blueprint from the Git repository, or create a Background Worker using this Dockerfile.
2. Set the non-generated secret values in the Render dashboard: `BOT_TOKEN`, `TELEGRAM_API_ID`, `TELEGRAM_API_HASH`, and `OWNER_IDS`.
3. Render creates `ENCRYPTION_KEY` from `render.yaml`; retain it across redeployments because it encrypts the saved worker session.
4. Provision persistent disk at `/app/data` if the selected Render plan supports it. Without persistent disk, session/database/progress will be lost on redeploy.
5. Deploy. The bot uses long polling, so no public domain or webhook route is required.

**Use Render Background Worker or a normal Linux VM for this app. Vercel is not a viable runtime for it:** Vercel functions are request-limited/serverless and do not provide a durable, always-running long-polling worker, persistent SQLite state, or reliable temporary large-file processing.

## Git preparation

```bash
git init
git add .
git status              # confirm .env and data/ are not staged
git commit -m "Initial Telegram media mirror bot"
git branch -M main
git remote add origin YOUR_REPOSITORY_URL
git push -u origin main
```

`.env`, SQLite databases, reports, session data, and temporary files are excluded by `.gitignore`.

## Project layout

```text
app/
  bot_ui.py             aiogram commands, menus, FSM project/session setup
  config.py             environment configuration
  crypto.py             encrypted session helper
  database.py           SQLite schema and transactional repository
  keyboards.py          inline bot menus
  main.py               polling process lifecycle and recovery
  models.py             project and transfer domain types
  reports.py            full-report generator
  telegram_gateway.py   Telethon login/session/chat validation
  worker.py             ordered download/re-upload worker and manager
tests/                  offline unit tests
Dockerfile
render.yaml
```
